  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Vertical projection of rotating radius OA onto the separate vertical O1X axis: preserve the original upper-left A, dashed circular locus and perpendicular horizontal guides, upward x vector, full-radius dimension x_m, angle phi from the positive vertical reference, and counterclockwise omega_0 direction. No numerical angle or radius is a source given.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-09.png",
      "sha256": "7697ad346d7cc1b8136ff648a1cca03605e824025ac4161afe26eeee81f68863"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n7",
        "text": "7. The relation between simple harmonic motion and circular motion\n\nFigure 9\n\nLet a radius vector rotate uniformly with angular speed $\\omega_0$, and represent it by $\\overrightarrow{OA}$.\nWe examine only its projection on the $O_1X$ axis: the corresponding position on that axis (Figure $9$).\nAt the starting instant ($t=0$), let the radius point in the positive direction of the $O_1X$ axis.\n\nThis initial alignment fixes the starting phase.\nAt that instant, the projection of $\\overrightarrow{OA}$ is longest and equals the radius length:\n$|\\overrightarrow{OA}|=x_{\\mathrm{m}}$.\n\nThat length is also the amplitude of the projection's back-and-forth motion.\nThe projection has a sign, showing which side of its equilibrium position it lies on.\n\nAs the radius vector $\\overrightarrow{OA}$ rotates uniformly, denote its angle from the $O_1X$ axis by $\\varphi$.\nStarting from the alignment just chosen, the accumulated rotation angle is proportional to elapsed time:\n$\\varphi=\\omega_0t$.\n\nThe angle here tracks the rotation continuously.\nIt is not limited to the smaller angle between two lines.\n\nOn the $O_1X$ axis, denote the radius vector's signed projection by $x$.\nIts length and direction depend on the rotation angle and can be described using cosine:\n$x=x_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\varphi=x_{\\mathrm{m}}\\cos\\omega_0t$.\n\nReplacing the angle by its time-dependent expression tells us how the projection changes with time.\n\nThis projection changes back and forth according to a cosine law, so its motion is simple harmonic.\nThe statement concerns the projection on the axis.\n\nThe ball itself continues around the circle;\nit does not move back and forth along that axis.\n\nWe can make this projection visible as a shadow.\nFix a ball to a disk that rotates uniformly, and shine parallel light toward it in a direction parallel to the disk's plane.\nPlace a screen perpendicular to the light's direction to receive the ball's shadow (Figure $10$).\n\nTake the projected position of the disk's center, $O_1$, as the origin for the shadow's displacement.\nThe shadow's upward and downward displacement then equals the projection of the radius vector $\\overrightarrow{OA}$ on a vertical axis.\nBecause that projection varies harmonically, the shadow moves up and down harmonically.\n\nThe ball moves around a circle.\nWe observe its projection, not a new straight-line motion of the ball itself.\n\nFigure 10\n\nHang a weight from a spring where the same parallel light can reach it.\nFirst place the weight at equilibrium and adjust its suspension so that its shadow is at the same height as the disk center's shadow.\nWe can then compare their upward and downward displacements from their respective equilibrium positions.\n\nMove the weight upward from equilibrium by a distance $x_{\\mathrm{m}}$, then release it without an extra push.\nChoose that distance to equal the radius of the ball's circular path, so the two shadows can have equal amplitudes.\nChoose the disk's rotation rate to match the weight's oscillation frequency, and start both motions together at their uppermost positions (Figure $11$).\n\nUnder these conditions, the two shadows can move together with matching positions and directions.\nTreat the spring's restoring force as linear and resistance as negligible during the observation.\n\n**The spring weight and the shadow of the uniformly rotating ball follow the same displacement law.**\n\nThe disk does not drive the spring.\nThis experiment compares their displacement laws.\nThe known harmonic motion of the ball's shadow illustrates the spring weight's harmonic motion.\n\nFigure 11"
      }
    ],
